    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the interview requirement.\n\nStep 1 — For each value, calculate the missing complement.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction twoSum(nums: readonly number[], target: number): [number, number] | null {\n  const indexByValue = new Map<number, number>();\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    const needed = target - nums[i]!;\n\n    if (indexByValue.has(needed)) {\n      return [indexByValue.get(needed)!, i];\n    }\n\n    indexByValue.set(nums[i]!, i);\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN baseline:\n```ts\nfunction twoSumUsingFindIndex(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n  target: number,\n): [number, number] | null {\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    const j = nums.findIndex(\n      (value, index) => index > i && value === target - nums[i]!,\n    );\n\n    if (j !== -1) return [i, j];\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n[2,7,11,15], target 9\n→ [0,1]\n\nComplexity:\nMap solution O(n) average time, O(n) space.\nNested/search baseline O(n²).\n\nInterview takeaway:\nHashing is often the trade-off: spend memory to remove repeated searching.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nExplain the invariant, complexity, edge cases, and why the optimization is correct.",
