    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the interview requirement.\n\nStep 1 — In an undirected graph, visiting an already-visited neighbor is a cycle only when that neighbor is not the parent.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction hasCycle(\n  graph: ReadonlyMap<number, readonly number[]>,\n): boolean {\n  const visited = new Set<number>();\n\n  function dfs(node: number, parent: number | null): boolean {\n    visited.add(node);\n\n    for (const neighbor of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {\n        if (dfs(neighbor, node)) return true;\n      } else if (neighbor !== parent) {\n        return true;\n      }\n    }\n\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  for (const node of graph.keys()) {\n    if (!visited.has(node) && dfs(node, null)) return true;\n  }\n\n  return false;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / iterative:\n```ts\nfunction hasCycleIterative(\n  graph: ReadonlyMap<number, readonly number[]>,\n): boolean {\n  const visited = new Set<number>();\n\n  for (const start of graph.keys()) {\n    if (visited.has(start)) continue;\n\n    const stack: Array<[number, number | null]> = [[start, null]];\n\n    while (stack.length > 0) {\n      const [node, parent] = stack.pop()!;\n      if (visited.has(node)) continue;\n\n      visited.add(node);\n\n      for (const neighbor of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n        if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {\n          stack.push([neighbor, node]);\n        } else if (neighbor !== parent) {\n          return true;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return false;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n1—2—3 has no cycle.\n1—2—3—1 has a cycle.\n\nComplexity:\nO(V + E) time and O(V) auxiliary space.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nExplain the invariant, complexity, edge cases, and why the optimization is correct.",
